Edges: $1 \to 2$, $2 \to 1$, $3 \to 1$.
The conditions are:
$x_1 \le x_2$
$x_2 \le x_1$
$x_3 \le x_1$
From $x_1 \le x_2$ and $x_2 \le x_1$, we must have $x_1 = x_2$.
Then $x_3 \le x_1$.
So we need to choose $x_1, x_2, x_3 \in \{1, 2, 3\}$ such that $x_1 = x_2$ and $x_3 \le x_1$.
Possible values for $x_1$:
If $x_1 = 1$, then $x_2 = 1$, $x_3 \in \{1\}$. (1 solution: 1,1,1)
If $x_1 = 2$, then $x_2 = 2$, $x_3 \in \{1, 2\}$. (2 solutions: 2,2,1; 2,2,2)
If $x_1 = 3$, then $x_2 = 3$, $x_3 \in \{1, 2, 3\}$. (3 solutions: 3,3,1; 3,3,2; 3,3,3)
