$L_1 = \{u \mid d(u)=1\}$.
$L_2 = \{u \mid d(u)=2\}$.
And so on.
The condition is $\sum_{i \text{ even}} |L_i| = \sum_{i \text{ odd}} |L_i|$.
Note that $L_0 \subseteq E$, $L_1 \subseteq O$, $L_2 \subseteq E$, etc.
So $|E| = |L_0| + |L_2| + \dots$ and $|O| = |L_1| + |L_3| + \dots$.
The condition is $\sum_{i \text{ even}} |L_i| = \sum_{i \text{ odd}} |L_i|$.

Let's consider the case where the graph is bipartite.
If the graph is bipartite, then there exists a partition of vertices $V = V_{even} \cup V_{odd}$ such that all edges go between $V_{even}$ and $V_{odd}$.
In this case, for any $u \in V_{even}$, $d(u)$ is even, and for any $u \in V_{odd}$, $d(u)$ is odd.
Thus $E = V_{even}$ and $O = V_{odd}$.
The condition $|E| = |O|$ becomes $|V_{even}| = |V_{odd}| = N/2$.
So, any bipartite graph with a balanced bipartition satisfies the condition.
Is it possible for a non-bipartite graph to satisfy the condition?
If a graph is not bipartite, it contains an odd cycle.
