The problem asks us to calculate the final rating of Takahashi after participating in $N$ contests, given $Q$ queries. Each query provides an initial rating $X$.

For each contest $i$ (where $1 \le i \le N$), there is a range $[L_i, R_i]$. If the current rating $R$ satisfies $L_i \le R \le R_i$, the rating increases by 1. Otherwise, it stays the same. The order of contests is fixed from 1 to $N$.

Let $f_i(X)$ be the rating after contest $i$ given an initial rating $X$.
$f_0(X) = X$.
$f_i(X) = f_{i-1}(X) + 1$ if $L_i \le f_{i-1}(X) \le R_i$, else $f_i(X) = f_{i-1}(X)$.
We need to compute $f_N(X)$ for various $X$.

Constraints:
$N \le 2 \times 10^5$
$R_i \le 5 \times 10^5$
$Q \le 3 \times 10^5$
$X \le 5 \times 10^5$

Let's analyze the condition $L_i \le f_{i-1}(X) \le R_i$.
Notice that the rating only increases. So $f_i(X) \ge f_{i-1}(X)$.
Also, the maximum possible rating is bounded. Since $X \le 5 \times 10^5$ and $N \le 2 \times 10^5$, the rating can go up to $5 \times 10^5 + 2 \times 10^5 = 7 \times 10^5$. However, the ranges $[L_i, R_i]$ are within $[1, 5 \times 10^5]$. If the rating exceeds $5 \times 10^5$, it will never satisfy $L_i \le \text{rating} \le R_i$ because $R_i \le 5 \times 10^5$. So once the rating exceeds $5 \times 10^5$, it stops increasing.

Let's look at the structure of the function $f_N(X)$.
Since the condition for increasing is based on the current rating, and the rating is non-decreasing, the function $f_N(X)$ is non-decreasing with respect to $X$. If $X_1 < X_2$, then $f_i(X_1) \le f_i(X_2)$ for all $i$.
